1309: \begin{proposition}[Machine-readable certificate records]
1310: \label{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}
1311: For the current presentation package, the finite record checker
1312: $V$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}, with the
1313: record-acceptance semantics of
1314: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, has an executable record-level
1315: instance over the review bundle, conditional on exact supplemental inclusion of
1316: \path{review_bundle/} in the completed submission with matching final digests, as stated in
1317: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}.  The schema file
1318: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}, the pass-record ledger
1319: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, the primary-interface map
1320: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json}, the primary-claim inventory
1321: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script
1322: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, and the recorded run
1323: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} instantiate the verifier at
1324: the declared evidence levels for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_7$.  The negative fixture
1325: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json} and recorded run
1326: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md} instantiate the first
1327: failure mode for a forbidden command-run escalation.  Each pass record in the
1328: ledger has the five fields \emph{gate name}, \emph{input surface},
1329: \emph{decision}, \emph{evidence pointer}, and \emph{evidence level}.  The
1330: machine-readable submission-interface map is locked to rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}
1331: of Table~\ref{tab:submission-interface-map} by its row identifiers, certificates,
1332: surfaces, and evidence levels; the primary-claim inventory is locked to rows
1333: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} by its row identifiers, source locations, row classes,
1334: and evidence levels.  These executable locks check the finite record interface;
1335: full semantic equality between prose paraphrases and mathematical meaning,
1336: soundness of the gate implementations, and path completeness beyond the declared
1337: surfaces remain outside the record-check claim and stay at the manual audit
1338: boundary stated in the row classes.
1339: \end{proposition}
1340: 
1341: \begin{proof}
1342: All objects in the statement are finite files in the submitted review bundle, so
1343: the claim is a finite record check.  The manifest
1344: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} contains top-level fields named
1345: \path{certificate_schema}, \path{current_package_pass_records},
1346: \path{submission_interface_map}, \path{primary_claim_inventory},
1347: \path{verifier_script}, and \path{verifier_run_log}; these fields point to the verifier
1348: bundle files named in the statement.  Thus the executable verifier is not an
1349: implicit prose convention but a submitted artifact surface.
1350: 
1351: The schema file lists the seven claim kinds, the two evidence levels, the same
1352: gate dispatch $\Gamma$ used in the supplement, and the required pass-record
1353: fields $(g,I,d,e,\ell)$.  The pass-record ledger has exactly seven entries,
1354: $a_1,\ldots,a_7$.  For each entry it records a nonempty claim-kind set, the
1355: path-verified evidence level, a finite evidence surface, the route-boundary or
1356: human-promotion field, pass records, the discovery-interface field, and the
1357: evidence boundary.  Inspecting the seven entries gives the required gate coverage:
1358: $a_1$ has inventory and marker-existence records; $a_2$ has promotion, overlap,
1359: submission-state, and submission-pack records; $a_3$ has promotion, overlap, and
1360: submission-state records; $a_4$ has promotion, overlap, submission-state, and
1361: submission-pack records; $a_5$ has pinned-discovery and theorem-content-delta
1362: records; and $a_6$ has status and limitation-ledger records.  These are exactly
1363: the unions of the gate sets assigned by $\Gamma$ to the claim-kind sets displayed
1364: in Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}.  The only row with discovery kind
1365: is $a_5$, and it is the only row with a non-null discovery-interface field; every
1366: path-verified row carries a boundary saying that no command-run assertion is
1367: made.  The seventh row $a_7$ is the bibliography/venue-freshness traceability and
1368: submission-pack record; it is path-verified in the acceptance ledger, while the
1369: separate command-boundary inventory below records the live-check command log used
1370: for the bounded venue and bibliography freshness assertion.
1371: 
1372: The script \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} implements precisely
1373: these finite checks: required manifest fields, entries $a_1$--$a_7$, kind-domain
1374: membership, evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, the five
1375: pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement, discovery-field discipline, the
1376: path-verified boundary condition, exact row locks for \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}
1377: against the submission-interface JSON fields, and row-class locks for
1378: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} against the primary-claim inventory locations, statuses,
1379: and evidence levels.  The recorded log
1380: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} records a successful run of
1381: that script over the submitted files and states the boundary that no Lean build,
1382: publication-daemon run, or Rule110 dynamic artifact rerun is being certified.
1383: The negative fixture log
1384: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md} records the command
1385: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}
1386: and the expected rejection of a route row whose evidence level is raised to
1387: command-run without the required command-run tuple.
1388: Consequently the executable artifact proves exactly the schema-level instance of
1389: $V$ claimed here.  By the schema-relative finite-record exactness of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, this is also
1390: an instance of the paper's finite record-acceptance semantics: the executable
1391: record accepts exactly the current-package certificates and rejects the
1392: recorded command-run escalation example; hence it supplies no stronger evidence
1393: level or omitted gate semantics.
1394: 
1395: Finally, \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json} has rows
1396: \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}.  The verifier now rejects a mutation of any row's
1397: primary claim text, certificate list, row-or-manifest surface list, or evidence
1398: level, so the JSON file is an executable finite mirror of the interface table at
1399: that declared field level.  The file
1400: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json} lists the concrete primary-note
1401: assertions as rows \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}; every load-bearing row names a
1402: certificate, row, manifest surface, or bounded-context status and declares an
1403: evidence level, and the verifier locks each row's identifier, source location,
1404: status class, and evidence level.  Thus the primary note has a complete finite
1405: claim inventory at the declared review fields, and no additional primary
1406: load-bearing assertion is licensed by the machine-readable interface.  The proof
1407: does not assert that the script decides semantic paraphrase equality or discovers
1408: undeclared paths; those remain the manual audit obligations represented by the
1409: table rows and by Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-inventory-freshness-criterion}.\end{proof}
1410: 
1411: \begin{theorem}[Executable record non-escalation]
1412: \label{thm:executable-record-non-escalation}
1413: Let $F$ be the finite field projection read by
1414: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}: manifest-field presence,
1415: entries $a_1$--$a_6$, claim-kind membership, evidence-level membership,
1416: assigned-gate coverage, pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement,
1417: discovery-field discipline, path-verified boundary status, row locks for
1418: \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, and row-class locks for \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}.  Assuming
1419: the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support surface of
1420: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, among all
1421: assertions about the current review bundle that are functions only of this
1422: projection, Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves the
1423: maximal executable claim: the six current-package certificates satisfy the finite
1424: record-acceptance relation of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} at the
1425: declared path-verified fields under the input--predicate--output contract of
1426: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, and no accepted row carries a
1427: command-run boundary.  Thus the bundled Python script is an implementation
1428: witness for the finite schema, not a separately formalized verifier-language
1429: semantics.  In particular, the executable verifier cannot certify any strictly
1430: stronger assertion whose truth depends on prose-to-semantics equality,
1431: independent extraction of claims from the manuscript, completeness of paths not
1432: declared in the records, or the external semantics of a Lean development,
1433: publication daemon, artifact suite, evaluator, or venue state.
1434: \end{theorem}
1435: 
1436: \begin{proof}
1437: By the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the
1438: script reads exactly the finite projection $F$ listed in the statement and rejects
1439: mutations of the locked fields.  Hence every executable assertion made by that
1440: script is constant on the equivalence classes of review bundles with the same
1441: projection $F$.  On this quotient, the six ledger entries, their kind sets, their
1442: evidence levels, their assigned gates, their pass-record fields, their discovery
1443: boundaries, and their path-verified non-command boundaries are all fixed.  By
1444: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, the executable checker has no
1445: other mathematical output channel.  The schema-relative finite-record acceptance exactness
1446: theorem then identifies precisely what this fixed data entails: acceptance of
1447: those six certificates in the finite relation
1448: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$ and rejection of stronger readings that add kinds, raise
1449: the evidence level, omit route or discovery fields, omit pass records, or assert
1450: command-run evidence without the required command tuple.
1451: 
1452: Now consider any proposed stronger assertion of one of the four types named in
1453: the statement.  Its truth is not determined by $F$.  Prose-to-semantics equality
1454: depends on interpreting manuscript language against mathematical meanings, while
1455: $F$ contains only row identifiers, selected text locks, classes, locations,
1456: certificate lists, surfaces, and evidence levels.  Independent extraction depends
1457: on an extraction procedure over the manuscript, while $F$ contains the submitted
1458: inventory rather than the result of an independently specified extractor.
1459: Completeness of undeclared paths depends on paths outside the finite surfaces
1460: read by the verifier.  External artifact semantics depends on running or
1461: semantically validating objects whose command, source state, environment, exit
1462: code, and log path are not present in the path-verified rows.  Therefore two
1463: packages can agree on $F$ while differing on such a stronger assertion.  Since a
1464: function of $F$ has the same value on both packages, no executable claim proved by
1465: this verifier can certify that stronger assertion.  The maximal executable
1466: record-level claim is exactly the one stated in
1467: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}.\end{proof}
1468: 
1469: \begin{theorem}[Executable certificate-construction invariant]
1470: \label{thm:executable-certificate-construction-invariant}
1471: For the submitted review bundle, let $G$ be the union of the finite fields read by
1472: the two executable verifiers
1473: \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py} and
1474: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  Thus $G$ contains the ten
1475: source-interface table triples, the JSON source-interface records, the pinned
1476: source snapshot path, digest, line count, declared source commit and line ranges,
1477: the six current-package pass-record entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, the submission-map
1478: rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, the primary-inventory row locks
1479: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}, and the two recorded successful verifier logs.  On the
1480: equivalence class of packages with the same projection $G$, the publication-safety
1481: interface has a unique executable finite certificate construction: the imported
1482: discovery-gate interface is the sixteen-row checked source interface of
1483: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, the accepted
1484: current-package certificates are exactly the six records of
1485: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, and any stronger
1486: reading that changes the source interface, adds an accepted row, raises the
1487: evidence level, adds a command-run assertion, or asserts external semantic or
1488: venue correctness is not determined by $G$ and is rejected at the finite-record
1489: boundary unless new certificate fields and verifier records are supplied.  This
1490: is a finite-record construction invariant: the scripts witness the listed
1491: predicate checks on bundled JSON and table fields, and no claim is made here that
1492: Python itself has been given a separate formal operational semantics.
1493: \end{theorem}
1494: 
1495: \begin{proof}
1496: The first executable verifier reads only the manuscript source-interface table,
1497: \path{source_interface_record.json}, the \path{pinned_source_snapshot} object of
1498: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and the bundled source snapshot.
1499: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} proves that these
1500: fields agree on the ordered sixteen triples, the original path, the source commit, the
1501: sixteen line ranges, the snapshot digest, the snapshot line count, and the containment
1502: of each imported label in its declared range.  Hence every package with the same
1503: projection $G$ has the same executable imported discovery-gate interface, and no
1504: table-only, JSON-only, manifest-only, stale-digest, stale-line-count, or
1505: out-of-range imported row can be accepted inside that projection.
1506: 
1507: The second executable verifier reads the manifest fields, the certificate schema,
1508: the six pass-record entries, the submission-interface map, and the primary-claim
1509: inventory.  Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves
1510: that these fields instantiate the finite verifier $V$ for exactly rows
1511: $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ at the path-verified level, with the displayed gate coverage,
1512: discovery-field discipline, row locks, and non-command boundary.  Therefore every
1513: package with the same projection $G$ has the same six accepted current-package
1514: certificates and the same absence of command-run evidence in those certificates.
1515: The input--predicate--output contract,
1516: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, says that these are the only
1517: positive outputs available to the certificate checker; the finite-record
1518: exactness theorem,
1519: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, identifies these accepted records
1520: with the record-acceptance relation and rejects stronger readings that require
1521: additional claim kinds, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery fields,
1522: pass rows, or command-run tuples.
1523: 
1524: Combining the two paragraphs gives the stated uniqueness on the $G$-projection:
1525: the discovery component of any accepted current-package claim is forced through
1526: the same checked ten-row source interface, and the publication component is forced
1527: through the same six checked certificate rows.  Any proposed stronger assertion
1528: listed in the statement either changes one of the finite fields of $G$, in which
1529: case it is not a package in the same equivalence class, or leaves $G$ unchanged,
1530: in which case the two verifiers receive identical inputs and can return only the
1531: same finite-record conclusions.  Such an unchanged projection contains no
1532: coordinate for external theorem truth, proof-assistant correctness, unlogged
1533: daemon execution, artifact semantic validity, or venue acceptance.  Those claims
1534: therefore require an enlarged certificate and additional executable records, and
1535: without them they are outside the accepted finite-record interface.\end{proof}
1536: 
1537: \begin{proposition}[Deterministic claim-kind dispatch]
1538: \label{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
1539: For every load-bearing manuscript claim whose kind lies in the seven-kind domain
1540: above, $\Gamma$ assigns a unique finite set of deterministic gates.  In a
1541: pipeline that is record-gate-sound for acceptance, a mixed load-bearing claim may be
1542: used as accepted work only if its acceptance entry records passage through every
1543: gate in the union assigned to its component kinds.
1544: \end{proposition}
1545: 
1546: \begin{proof}
1547: The seven atomic clauses in the definition of $\Gamma$ assign one explicitly named
1548: finite gate set to each atomic claim kind.  Hence an atomic claim has a unique
1549: assigned gate set.  For a mixed claim, the component-kind set is finite because
1550: it is a subset of the seven-kind domain; taking the union of the corresponding
1551: finite gate sets is deterministic and finite.  Record-gate-soundness for acceptance
1552: requires the acceptance entry of a load-bearing claim to record passage through
1553: every gate assigned by $\Gamma$.  If a component gate of a mixed claim were
1554: omitted, then the projection of the claim to that component kind would lack its
1555: assigned pass record.  Such an entry would not be record-gate-sound.  Therefore accepted
1556: mixed claims in a record-gate-sound pipeline must pass every gate in the union.\end{proof}
1557: 
1558: \begin{theorem}[Mixed-claim projection obstruction]
1559: \label{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction}
1560: Let $c$ be a load-bearing mixed claim with nonempty component-kind set
1561: $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$ and submitted candidate certificate
1562: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$.  For a nonempty subset $J\subseteq K$, write
1563: \[
1564: G_J=\bigcup_{k\in J}\Gamma(k)
1565: \]
1566: and let $c_J$ denote the projected reading of $c$ that keeps exactly the
1567: claim-kind components in $J$ and keeps the same declared evidence level,
1568: evidence surface, promotion boundary, and command boundary, except that the
1569: discovery field is required precisely when discovery lies in $J$.  Then the
1570: following are equivalent for the projected reading $c_J$:
1571: \begin{enumerate}
1572: \item the finite verifier accepts the projected certificate
1573: $\mathcal C_J=(J,\ell,E,H,R|_{G_J},D_J,B)$, where $R|_{G_J}$ is the restriction of
1574: $R$ to rows whose gate name lies in $G_J$ and $D_J$ is $D$ exactly when discovery
1575: lies in $J$ and is absent otherwise;
1576: \item every gate $g\in G_J$ has in $R$ a pass record with the five required
1577: fields and with an allowed evidence pointer, the retained boundary fields $E$,
1578: $H$, $D_J$, and $B$ satisfy the corresponding schema clauses at level $\ell$, and
1579: $J\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$.
1580: \end{enumerate}
1581: Consequently, if an acceptance entry for $c$ records passage only through a
1582: proper gate subset $Q\subset\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, then every component
1583: subset $J$ with $\neg(G_J\subseteq Q)$ has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord,
1584: namely a missing pass record for some gate in $G_J\setminus Q$.  In particular,
1585: no passage through route, submission, scheduler, artifact, traceability,
1586: formal-source, or discovery gates can certify a mixed claim's remaining component
1587: unless the pass rows assigned to that remaining component are also present.
1588: \end{theorem}
1589: 
1590: \begin{proof}
1591: The projected certificate $\mathcal C_J$ is a certificate of the same schema as
1592: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}, with claim-kind set
1593: $J$ in place of $K$ and with gate field restricted to the finite set $G_J$.
1594: Because $J\subseteq K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
1595: assigns exactly the finite gate set $G_J$ to the projected reading.  Applying the
1596: schema-relative exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness},
1597: to $\mathcal C_J$ gives the equivalence: acceptance is exactly satisfaction of
1598: the six finite schema clauses for the retained fields.  Clause A1 is the domain
1599: condition $J\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$ and $\ell\in\mathsf{Lev}$; clause A2
1600: checks the evidence surface $E$; clause A3 checks that each gate in $G_J$ has a
1601: pass record in $R|_{G_J}$ with an allowed pointer; clause A4 checks the retained
1602: promotion boundary $H$; clause A5 checks precisely the retained discovery field
1603: $D_J$; and clause A6 checks the retained command boundary $B$.  These are exactly
1604: the conditions in item~2, so items~1 and~2 are equivalent.
1605: 
1606: Now suppose the row for the mixed claim records only pass rows with gate names in
1607: a proper subset $Q$ of the full assigned gate set.  Choose a nonempty
1608: $J\subseteq K$ with $\neg(G_J\subseteq Q)$ and choose $g\in G_J\setminus Q$.  The
1609: projected verifier for $c_J$ must check $g$ by clause A3, because $g$ is assigned
1610: to the retained component-kind set $J$.  No row for $g$ is present among the
1611: recorded gate names in $Q$, so clause A3 fails.  The verifier reads only the
1612: finite fields consisting of $J$, $\Gamma$, the absent gate name $g$, and the gate
1613: row field $R$; deleting fields not read by this failed check leaves an
1614: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of the second obstruction type in
1615: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  Since the argument
1616: uses only membership in the assigned gate set, it applies uniformly to each of
1617: the seven atomic gate interfaces.  Thus one component's gate passage cannot be
1618: substituted for another component's missing assigned pass rows in a mixed
1619: load-bearing claim.\end{proof}
1620: 
1621: \begin{theorem}[Assigned-gate soundness audit boundary]
1622: \label{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}
1623: Let $P$ be a finite submitted package with pass-record ledger $L$, dispatch
1624: table $\Gamma$, and submitted support surface $S$.  For a load-bearing claim
1625: $c$, write $G(c)=\bigcup_{k\in K(c)}\Gamma(k)$ for the finite set of gates
1626: assigned to its claim-kind set.  The package may use a ledger row for $c$ as a
1627: sound record-gate witness only through the finite predicate
1628: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.
1629: Equivalently, the available finite-record strength is exactly the following:
1630: \begin{enumerate}
1631: \item for every $g\in G(c)$ the submitted ledger contains a pass row for $g$,
1632: including the gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence
1633: level;
1634: \item every evidence pointer named by those pass rows denotes a submitted path,
1635: manifest row, theorem/proposition/table label, uploaded bundle file, or explicitly
1636: declared historical boundary at the evidence level stated in the row; and
1637: \item if the manuscript asserts any stronger proposition that the underlying gate
1638: implementation was independently sound, freshly executed, or semantically correct
1639: beyond the recorded finite schema check, the submitted package also contains a
1640: gate-soundness audit tuple for that stronger proposition: source path, command,
1641: source state, environment, exit code, and log path for the assigned gate.
1642: \end{enumerate}
1643: Consequently, in the absence of the audit tuple in (3), record-gate soundness is
1644: an explicit hypothesis of the publication-safety theorem, not a theorem proved by
1645: the current package.  The finite pass rows prove only the package-level statement
1646: that no accepted ledger claim omits an assigned gate row or its declared
1647: submitted evidence surface.
1648: \end{theorem}
1649: 
1650: \begin{proof}
1651: The dispatch table has finite values by Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch};
1652: hence $G(c)$ is finite.  A submitted pass-record ledger and support surface are
1653: also finite.  The predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ therefore expands to a
1654: finite conjunction of row and pointer tests: for each accepted load-bearing claim
1655: it supplies a certificate satisfying $V(c,\mathcal C)=1$, and for each gate name
1656: in $G(c)$ it searches the rows attached to $c$ and compares the finite fields
1657: gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence level.  If
1658: one of these rows is missing, the certificate contains the second bad subrecord
1659: of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, namely a missing
1660: pass record for an assigned gate.  If a row is present, the verifier has only the
1661: finite string and path fields carried by that row.
1662: 
1663: The second condition is exactly the local-checkability condition for the row's
1664: evidence pointer.  A pointer to a path, manifest row, theorem/proposition/table
1665: label, uploaded bundle file, or explicit historical boundary is a coordinate of
1666: the submitted finite record surface.  If the pointer does not denote one of
1667: those finite surfaces at the stated evidence level, the certificate has the first
1668: bad subrecord of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, a
1669: missing evidence surface.  Thus conditions (1) and (2) are precisely the
1670: submitted-row and submitted-pointer clauses of $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$, and they are
1671: precisely what the package can verify from $L$ and the submitted surfaces.
1672: 
1673: The third condition concerns a different claim kind.  External implementation
1674: soundness, fresh execution, and semantic correctness assert facts about the
1675: operation of a gate outside the row's finite fields.  By the evidence-level
1676: definitions and by Theorem~\ref{thm:executable-record-non-escalation}, a
1677: command-result claim is not supplied by a path-verified pass row; it requires a
1678: command tuple with source state, environment, exit code, and log path.  By
1679: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two worlds that have
1680: identical submitted pass rows but different unsubmitted gate implementations are
1681: indistinguishable to the finite verifier.  Hence no proof from (1) and (2) can
1682: establish the stronger external-gate proposition.  The stronger proposition is
1683: accepted only after the package is enlarged by the audit tuple in (3).  Without
1684: that enlargement, record-gate soundness remains the explicit finite predicate
1685: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$, while the proved package fact is exactly the absence of
1686: missing assigned-gate rows and missing declared evidence surfaces.\end{proof}
1687: 
1688: \begin{theorem}[Finite certificate obstruction skeleton]
1689: \label{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
1690: Let $c$ be a load-bearing manuscript claim whose component kinds lie in the
1691: seven-kind domain.  The verifier of
1692: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} gives the following
1693: complete audit classification at the declared evidence level.  A certificate for
1694: $c$ passes the publication-safety interface if and only if it has no bad
1695: subrecord of the following five types:
1696: \begin{enumerate}
1697: \item missing evidence surface for the declared evidence level;
1698: \item missing pass record for a gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$;
1699: \item missing human-promotion record when the claim crosses from intake or
1700: source material into active publication work;
1701: \item discovery escalation without the pinned discovery-gate source-interface
1702: rows for certificate, ledger, lineage, and boundary witnesses; or
1703: \item command-run escalation without command, source commit, environment, exit
1704: code, and log path.
1705: \end{enumerate}
1706: Equivalently, every unsafe accepted claim contains an inclusion-minimal finite
1707: bad subrecord of one of these five types, and deleting all other fields preserves
1708: the same rejection by the verifier.  This is an audit-skeleton theorem for the
1709: software-workshop route: it classifies finite certificate failures and makes no
1710: claim about theorem-proving complexity or about fresh execution of the
1711: underlying workflow.
1712: \end{theorem}
1713: 
1714: \begin{proof}
1715: Let $K$ be the finite set of component kinds of $c$.  Since $K$ is a subset of
1716: the seven-kind domain, Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch} gives a unique
1717: finite required gate set
1718: \[
1719: G(c)=\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k).
1720: \]
1721: The certificate field $R$ is indexed by exactly this finite set, so checking
1722: gate passage is a finite lookup: each $g\in G(c)$ must have a pass record and the
1723: record must cite an item of the finite evidence surface $E$.  The evidence-level
1724: field $\ell$ and boundary field $B$ make the same lookup finite for evidence
1725: strength: at path-verified level the certificate must name the source, artifact,
1726: pinned commit, local publication path, or gate-decision surface used by the
1727: claim; at command-run level it must additionally name command, source commit,
1728: environment, exit code, and log path.  Neither lookup interprets artifact
1729: semantics, reruns Lean, or predicts editorial outcomes; those are stronger claim
1730: kinds or stronger evidence levels that require their own records.  The human-promotion field $H$ separates
1731: the route boundary from all other gates: it is either a concrete promotion
1732: record or a finite marker that no such boundary is crossed.  Finally, when
1733: discovery lies in $K$, the discovery field $D$ names the pinned source-interface
1734: rows certified by Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
1735: Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface} and
1736: \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}; these rows are finite and
1737: therefore locally checkable in the same way as the other certificate fields.
1738: Thus the certificate is sufficient for a reviewer to check safety by examining a
1739: finite list of fields and cited artifacts, not by trusting generated prose.
1740: 
1741: It remains to prove that the displayed obstruction list is complete.  Suppose an
1742: accepted claim fails the publication-safety interface.  Choose an inclusion-minimal
1743: subrecord of its acceptance entry that still witnesses failure.  The certificate
1744: schema has exactly seven coordinates, and the first two of them are not extra
1745: obstruction families.  The claim-kind coordinate $K$ only determines the finite
1746: gate set $G(c)$ through $\Gamma$; once $K$ lies in the seven-kind domain, any
1747: failure caused by that determination is a missing assigned gate and hence type~2.
1748: The evidence-level coordinate $\ell$ only chooses between the path-verified and
1749: command-run interpretations of the boundary field; its failures therefore appear
1750: either as absent evidence at the declared level, type~1, or as absent command-run
1751: coordinates, type~5.  Thus every possible failure must be witnessed in one of
1752: the five substantive schema fields $E,R,H,D,B$.
1753: 
1754: If the failing field is the evidence surface $E$, the claim is not tied to a
1755: submitted path, bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit
1756: historical boundary at the declared level.  The minimal subrecord is therefore
1757: type~1.  If the failing field is the gate-record field $R$, some gate in the
1758: deterministic finite set $G(c)$ has no pass record, no decision, or no evidence
1759: pointer lying in the submitted surface; this is type~2.  If the failing field is
1760: the human-promotion field $H$, then all non-route evidence may be present but the
1761: claim has crossed from intake or source material into active publication work
1762: without the required human promotion marker; this is type~3.  If the failing
1763: field is the discovery field $D$, then a discovery or classifier-shift reading is
1764: being inferred from statement-only, code-only, mechanical, compression-only, or
1765: classifier-scored material without the pinned certificate, ledger, lineage, and
1766: boundary rows; this is type~4.  Finally, if the failing field is the boundary
1767: field $B$, the record either raises path-verified evidence to command-run
1768: strength without command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path, or
1769: omits the explicit no-command-run boundary; this is type~5.
1770: 
1771: The preceding paragraph exhausts the schema fields: $K$ and $\ell$ reduce to the
1772: same five substantive checks, and $E,R,H,D,B$ are precisely the five displayed
1773: obstruction types.  Minimality removes all unread fields and leaves exactly one
1774: of the five obstruction skeletons.  Conversely, if no bad subrecord of type~1--5
1775: is present, then each schema check in
1776: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} succeeds: the evidence
1777: surface is present at the declared level, every required gate has its recorded
1778: pass, the route-boundary field is correct, discovery rows are present exactly
1779: when required, and command-run strength is not asserted without command-run
1780: fields.  Hence the verifier accepts the certificate and the publication-safety
1781: interface holds for $c$ at the declared evidence level.
1782: 
1783: The final sentence follows from the construction.  The verifier reads only the
1784: finite certificate fields and the finite dispatch table, so the theorem
1785: classifies certificate failures for review.  It does not assert a lower bound,
1786: complexity separation, automated theorem-proving method, or fresh run of any
1787: external command.\end{proof}
1788: 
1789: \begin{theorem}[Finite extension-budget normal form]
1790: \label{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}
1791: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$, the finite certificate schema, and an accepted
1792: finite certificate
1793: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) for a load-bearing package claim $c$.
1794: Let $c^{+}$ be a proposed stronger reading of the same row which is not
1795: record-equivalent to $c$ in the sense of
1796: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Then $c^{+}$ is
1797: accepted by the publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted finite
1798: record surface contains an extension record in the following normal form:
1799: \[
1800: \Delta(c,c^{+})=(\Delta K,\Delta E,\Delta H,\Delta R,\Delta D,\Delta B),
1801: \]
1802: where the nonempty coordinates are exactly the coordinates needed by the stronger
1803: reading:
1804: \begin{enumerate}
1805: \item $\Delta K$ names every additional claim-kind component asserted by
1806: $c^{+}$;
1807: \item $\Delta E$ supplies the submitted evidence surfaces for those added
1808: components at the declared evidence level;
1809: \item $\Delta R$ supplies pass records for every gate in
1810: \(\bigcup_{k\in K\cup\Delta K}\Gamma(k)\) not already supplied by $R$;
1811: \item $\Delta H$ supplies any newly asserted promotion, route, venue, or external
1812: instantiation boundary;
1813: \item $\Delta D$ supplies the pinned discovery-source rows whenever the stronger
1814: reading adds discovery, classifier-shift, or theorem-content-delta content; and
1815: \item $\Delta B$ supplies the command, source-state, environment, exit-code, and
1816: log-path tuple whenever the stronger reading raises a path-verified row to
1817: command-run strength.
1818: \end{enumerate}
1819: Equivalently, every genuine strengthening has positive finite extension budget:
1820: at least one coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\) must be nonempty, and every
1821: nonempty obstruction class from
1822: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} must be paid by the
1823: corresponding coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\).  Without this extension record,
1824: the stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord and remains outside
1825: the certificate constructed in
1826: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.
1827: \end{theorem}
1828: 
1829: \begin{proof}
1830: First suppose that the extension record is present.  Enlarge the certificate by
1831: replacing its fields with
1832: \[
1833: (K\cup\Delta K,\ell,E\cup\Delta E,H\cup\Delta H,
1834: R\cup\Delta R,D\cup\Delta D,B\cup\Delta B),
1835: \]
1836: where the unions mean finite row union with the original row identifiers retained.
1837: The six clauses of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} are
1838: then exactly the six requirements displayed in the statement.  Clause~A1 reads
1839: the enlarged kind set and evidence level.  Clause~A2 reads the enlarged evidence
1840: surface.  Clause~A3 reads the enlarged gate-pass field and checks every gate
1841: assigned by $\Gamma$ to the enlarged kind set.  Clause~A4 reads the enlarged
1842: promotion and boundary field.  Clause~A5 reads the enlarged discovery-interface
1843: field exactly when discovery is present in the enlarged kind set.  Clause~A6
1844: reads the enlarged evidence-boundary field and accepts command-run strength only
1845: when the command tuple is present.  Therefore
1846: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} gives
1847: \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c^{+},\mathcal C\cup\Delta)\), and the finite interface
1848: accepts the stronger reading at precisely the declared evidence level.
1849: 
1850: Conversely, suppose $c^{+}$ is accepted.  Since $c^{+}$ is not
1851: record-equivalent to $c$, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
1852: implies that some finite coordinate read by the verifier differs from the
1853: coordinate of \(\mathcal C(c)\).  Collect the differences in the six displayed
1854: components: additional kinds in $\Delta K$, additional submitted surfaces in
1855: $\Delta E$, additional gate rows in $\Delta R$, additional promotion or external
1856: boundary fields in $\Delta H$, additional discovery rows in $\Delta D$, and
1857: additional evidence-boundary or command-run data in $\Delta B$.  These are finite
1858: because the verifier reads only finite records and the dispatch table has finite
1859: domain.  Acceptance of $c^{+}$ forces each changed coordinate to satisfy the
1860: corresponding clause A1--A6, so the collected difference record has the normal
1861: form stated above.
1862: 
1863: It remains only to prove positivity and obstruction completeness.  If every
1864: coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\) were empty, then $c$ and $c^{+}$ would induce the
1865: same values on all verifier coordinates and would be record-equivalent, contrary
1866: to hypothesis.  Thus any genuine strengthening has positive finite extension
1867: budget.  If some nonempty obstruction class is not supplied by its corresponding
1868: coordinate, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} identifies
1869: the first failed finite field as one of the five bad-subrecord types: missing
1870: evidence surface, missing gate record, missing promotion or external boundary,
1871: missing discovery rows, or unsupported command-run escalation.  Removing all
1872: unread fields leaves an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord.  Hence a stronger
1873: reading is accepted exactly when its finite extension budget is paid in submitted
1874: coordinates, and otherwise remains outside the original certificate.\end{proof}
1875: 
1876: \begin{proposition}[Gate-interface separation]
1877: \label{prop:gate-interface-separation}
1878: The promotion, overlap, submission-state, and theorem-content delta gates are
1879: separate deterministic interfaces.  None can be replaced by passage of another
1880: one when a load-bearing claim has the corresponding component.
1881: \end{proposition}
1882: 
1883: \begin{proof}
1884: Each named gate has a distinct invariant and evidence surface in the operational
1885: definition.  Promotion asks whether a candidate has crossed the human
1886: publication boundary; its evidence is the active-paper directory and pipeline
1887: metadata.  Overlap asks whether the route duplicates or conflicts with a sibling
1888: route; its evidence is route identity, overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths,
1889: and board state.  Submission-state asks whether the proposed publication action
1890: is compatible with submission markers, venue state, and submission-pack metadata.
1891: The theorem-content delta gate asks whether an asserted extension contains
1892: load-bearing theorem growth; its evidence is an inventory, hard-lint surface, or
1893: delta record.  These predicates are logically independent at the interface
1894: level: an active paper can still overlap a submitted sibling, a route with no
1895: overlap can still have an impermissible submission state, and a compiling edit in
1896: a permissible route can still lack theorem-content delta.  Therefore a
1897: load-bearing claim with any one of these components must pass that component's
1898: own gate, and passage through the others cannot supply the missing evidence.\end{proof}
1899: 
1900: \begin{corollary}[Human promotion is not daemon-visible active-track equivalence]
1901: \label{cor:human-promotion-daemon-active-track-separation}
1902: In the operational interface of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface},
1903: a human-promotion record and daemon-visible active-paper markers are not
1904: interchangeable certificate fields.  More precisely, let $c$ be any
1905: route-boundary claim whose accepted certificate is
1906: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$.  The certificate may be read as saying that a
1907: candidate is admitted to the publication-facing package only when the promotion
1908: field $H$ records the human editorial act and the corresponding route-gate pass
1909: record in $R$ names the active-paper marker surface required by the promotion
1910: gate.  A record containing only a human-promotion note but no active-paper marker
1911: surface does not certify daemon visibility, and a record containing only a
1912: daemon-visible \path{2026_*} marker surface but no human-promotion field does not
1913: certify editorial promotion.  In particular, the intake-isolation row $a_3$ of
1914: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
1915: supports exactly the bounded lesson that the intake packet remains outside
1916: daemon-visible publication work until both sides of the promotion interface are
1917: recorded; it does not identify human promotion with daemon enumeration.
1918: \end{corollary}
1919: 
1920: \begin{proof}
1921: Clause A4 of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} assigns the
1922: route-boundary information to the certificate coordinate $H$.  The route-gate
1923: part of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} is a different
1924: finite coordinate: its pass record belongs to $R$ and its evidence surface is the
1925: active-paper marker surface, namely a human-promotion act producing a
1926: \path{2026_*} directory with \path{main.tex} and \path{PIPELINE.md}.  Thus the
1927: schema checks two typed fields, not one untyped publication-status fact.  By
1928: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the finite verifier accepts a
1929: route-boundary certificate exactly when all required finite schema predicates
1930: hold.  If the active-paper marker surface is missing, then the route-gate pass
1931: record lacks its required evidence pointer in $R$; by
1932: Theorem~\ref{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction} this is an
1933: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord for the daemon-visible reading.  If the
1934: human-promotion field is missing, then A4 fails for the route-boundary crossing;
1935: this is a distinct missing-coordinate obstruction in $H$.  Proposition~\ref{prop:gate-interface-separation}
1936: prevents repairing either bad subrecord by passage through overlap,
1937: submission-state, scheduler, discovery, artifact, or theorem-content gates.
1938: 
1939: For row $a_3$, the acceptance ledger names the intake seed and active-paper
1940: detector paths, while the certificate ledger records the marker that the intake
1941: seed does not cross into publication work before promotion.  The case-witness
1942: table states the same failed active-paper invariant and detector block.  Applying
1943: the preceding paragraph to these finite coordinates gives the advertised bounded
1944: reading: inactive intake is separated from publication work by the promotion
1945: interface, but the certificate does not collapse the editorial act and daemon
1946: marker enumeration into a single equivalent notion.\end{proof}
1947: 
1948: \begin{proposition}[Scheduler selection is advisory before discovery gating]
1949: \label{prop:scheduler-selection-advisory}
1950: Let $s$ be a scheduler or critical-path score attached to a candidate claim
1951: $c$.  If $s$ recommends selecting $c$, then the recommendation is a
1952: work-selection claim and by itself can only pass the scheduler gate.  If $c$ is
1953: also asserted to be a discovery or classifier-shifting contribution in a
1954: record-gate-sound pipeline, then the accepted mixed claim must pass both the scheduler
1955: gate and the discovery gate.
1956: Consequently, critical-path scores may prioritize review order, and the imported
1957: classifier-shift selection rule may break ties among otherwise equal discovery
1958: potential coordinates, but neither score nor recommendation accepts a
1959: classifier-shift discovery unless the discovery gate also supplies the required
1960: certificate, ledger, and lineage witnesses.
1961: \end{proposition}
1962: 
1963: \begin{proof}
1964: By the definition of $\Gamma$, a scheduler recommendation has claim kind
1965: work-selection, so its atomic gate is the scheduler gate.  The scheduler gate's
1966: evidence surface records a score, backlog window, or analogous critical-path
1967: priority; none of these fields is a semantic naming certificate, a ledger
1968: witness, a DNA-complete theorem genome, a score certificate for a discovery
1969: claim, a lineage witness, or a not-claimed boundary.  Hence scheduler passage
1970: alone records only that $c$ is an eligible or prioritized work item.
1971: 
1972: Now suppose that the same recorded assertion also says that $c$ is a discovery
1973: or classifier-shifting contribution.  Then the assertion is mixed: it has a
1974: work-selection component and a discovery component.  Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
1975: therefore requires passage through every gate in the union assigned by $\Gamma$,
1976: namely the scheduler gate for the work-selection component and the pinned
1977: discovery gate for the discovery component.  The source-interface record in
1978: Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} includes the imported classifier-change
1979: selection theorem only in its bounded form: with positive classifier-change
1980: weight and all other coordinates fixed, such a selection operator favors the
1981: classifier-changing candidate.  The same source-interface record also includes
1982: pipeline safety, which requires certificate, ledger, genome, score, lineage, and
1983: boundary witnesses for accepted discovery.  Combining these two imported
1984: interfaces gives the stated conclusion: scheduling and classifier-shift scoring
1985: can advise selection order, but discovery acceptance still occurs only through
1986: the discovery gate.\end{proof}
1987: 
1988: The next result is the concrete verifier/interface contribution for the workshop
1989: route.  It constructs a finite certificate from submitted rows, proves that the
1990: certificate is exact for the acceptance relation above, and classifies all
1991: properly stronger readings by the preorder of
1992: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  Its sharpness is the finite
1993: extension-budget normal form of
1994: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, not an
1995: implementation-soundness or semantic-correctness theorem for the underlying
1996: tools.  The load-bearing trust boundary is hypothesis~(2), record-gate
1997: soundness: without the finite predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ tying
1998: ledger entries to assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers, the
1999: theorem has no certificate constructor to apply.  The schema-relative exactness
2000: and finite-coordinate classifier of
2001: Theorems~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} and
2002: \ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} then ensure that the resulting
2003: certificate proves only the finite-record claim it records.
